[2026-08-01 03:21:55] [cot:thought] being drafted around the central question of who bears the cost of climate damage and adaptation before policy catches up. Key figures include a $215???387 billion annual adaptation need, international public adaptation finance of roughly $28 billion in 2022, and a $187???359 billion annual gap.
[2026-08-01 03:21:55] [cot:thought] The Glasgow doubling target would close only about five percent of that gap. Climate finance totals approximately $1.46 trillion in 2022 and near $2 trillion by 2024, while adaptation spending has plateaued near $63???65 billion.
[2026-08-01 03:21:55] [cot:thought] Swiss Re reports a $424 billion protection gap in 2025 and a resilience index near 27 percent.
[2026-08-01 03:21:55] [cot:thought] Residual risk falls
[2026-08-01 03:21:55] [cot:thought] on uninsured households, developing-country budgets, insurers, and taxpayers through disaster relief. Five dashboard panels are planned: a needs-versus-flows waterfall showing the adaptation finance gap; a time series of international public adaptation finance from 2016 to 2022; a stacked pie chart of climate finance composition by mitigation, adaptation, and dual focus; a Sankey-style stacked view of residual risk allocation across insured and uninsured losses; and a country or region ranking of protection gaps. A
[2026-08-01 03:21:55] [cot:thought] year-range toggle will serve as the first control. A second control will let users choose between mid and high adaptation needs scenarios. A third control will filter by region and loss year. The planned chart types include a time-series area or line for flows, a horizontal bar for gap comparisons, a stacked bar for mitigation versus adaptation, and a scatter or composed view for the protection gap. The smoke test discovery logic and the end of the PostVisualization switch are also being reviewed.
